  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following polynomials are given in standard form?\n\nI. ${x}^{7}+5{x}^{2}-7{x}^{5}$\n\nII. ${x}^{8}+9{x}^{4}+3{x}^{6}$\n\nIII. ${x}^{7}+5{x}^{2}-7$",
  "worked_solution": "A polynomial is written in standard form when the terms are arranged in descending order of the variable's exponent. The first term should have the largest exponent, and the last term should have the smallest exponent.\n\nLet's analyze each polynomial.\n\n- Polynomial I is *not* in standard form. The highest exponent term is ${x}^{7},$ followed by $-7{x}^{5},$ and finally $5{x}^{2}.$ So, the standard form is ${x}^{7}-7{x}^{5}+5{x}^{2},$ which is different from the given arrangement.\n\n- Polynomial II is *not* in standard form. The highest exponent term is ${x}^{8},$ followed by $3{x}^{6},$ and finally $9{x}^{4}.$ So, the standard form is ${x}^{8}+3{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4},$ which is different from the given arrangement.\n\n- Polynomial III is in standard form. The highest exponent term is ${x}^{7}$ followed by $5{x}^{2},$ and finally $-7.$ So, the standard form is indeed ${x}^{7}+5{x}^{2}-7.$\n\nIn conclusion, only polynomial III is given in standard form.",
